  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 62-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer on checkpoint immunotherapy developed presumed immunotherapy pneumonitis requiring multiple courses of prednisone (each more than 1 month long), most recently completed 2 months ago. A CT chest now shows diffuse bronchial wall thickening and tree-in-bud opacities. She remains dry-coughing and dyspneic off steroids. Patient has a history of positive quantiferon with negative AFB testing x 3 about 5 years prior. What dose of steroids should be restarted and what is an appropriate taper plan?",
    "recommendations": "Do not resume systemic steroids at this time. Given the imaging pattern and history of a positive QuantiFERON (with prior negative AFBs), infection, particularly TB or MAI, must be excluded before re-escalating immunosuppression. Collect three induced sputum samples for AFB smear and culture over consecutive mornings, along with sputum culture for bacteria, fungus, and Pneumocystis jirovecii, and order a COVID-19/respiratory viral panel PCR. Urgently refer to pulmonary clinic and defer steroids until infectious etiologies have been ruled out and a true inflammatory pneumonitis is confirmed. Pulmonology may pursue bronchoscopy with BAL if induced sputum is negative."
  },
